Q: What idioms exist to make extensible options structs in Rust? A: <p>I think the most commonly used technique is to use an options struct with chainable setter methods:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>Options::new().a(5).c(3).build();
</code></pre>
<p>A great example of this sort of thing is <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/1.93.0/std/process/struct.Command.html#examples-4" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>process::Command</code></a> (I linked to an example of a complex use of it).</p>
<p>The options struct itself can be implemented something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>#[derive(…)] // whatever traits seem appropriate
struct Options {
    a: u32, // private variable
    c: u32
}

impl Options {
  fn new() -&gt; Self { … }
  fn a(&amp;mut self, a: u32) -&gt; &amp;mut Self { self.a = a; self }
  …
}
</code></pre>
<p>Using <code>&amp;mut self</code> like this gives this sort of ergonomic syntax only when the options are used immediately (which is why I added a <code>build()</code> to the example above); you run into lifetime problems if you try to return the result of the chain. If you use this technique and a user wants to store an options structure for future use, they'd have to write the code less ergonomically:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>let mut options = Options::new();
options.a(5).c(3);
return options;
</code></pre>
<p>but it still isn't too bad. If you want to allow chained method syntax to produce a value that can be returned, you can do so by using <code>self</code> or <code>Box&lt;self&gt;</code> instead of <code>&amp;mut self</code> (although both have slightly worse performance, except in the case where the options struct is very small and thus <code>self</code> is OK).</p>
<p>Although you can simplify the repetitive code with an external crate or macro, it isn't really worth it.</p>
<p>This technique also has the advantage that you can change the way the options are represented internally without breaking backwards compatibility (by putting logic into the setters), or, e.g., have setting one option disable a different option.</p>

Q: Is it possible to deprecate a particular class template specialization? A: <p>You can use the <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/attributes/deprecated" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>[[deprecated]]</code></a> in the said explicit specialization(s) as shown below:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T, int N=8&gt; struct C
{

};
template&lt;&gt; struct [[deprecated]] C&lt;bool, 5&gt;{};
C&lt; bool, 5&gt; obj_1; //gives warning 


template&lt;typename T&gt; struct C&lt;T&gt;{};

C&lt;int&gt; obj_2; //no warning 
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/4PabExx5W" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Working demo</a>. This works with all three compilers(gcc, clang and msvc). With this gcc will generate the following warning:</p>
<pre><code>warning: 'C' is deprecated [-Wdeprecated-declarations]
    6 | C&lt; bool, 5&gt; obj_1; //gives warning
      |             ^~~~
</code></pre>
<p>Note that with msvc you'll need to use <code>/W3</code>.</p>
